        comment     2026-11-12T10:34:58Z
            Rules for every brief (from core-task-briefs-2026-11-12.md, proposed)

            - **Spec first.** The seven runtime Specs in `workflow/specs/core/` (WriteLanes, Actors, WriteAhead, 
              Restore,
              Reads, Turn, Codec) are the build's source. Where a Spec and the code disagree, the Spec wins; where a 
              Spec is
              silent, stop and ask. Shared terms: ending, Attempt, lane and owed row are WriteLanes'; Start and Life are
              Actors'; record kinds are WriteAhead R4 and R5.
            - **Every requirement is a failing check first.** Write the check and its wrong runtime, see the check red 
              on
              today's code (or, where today's code is already right, the wrong runtime red and the check green), commit
              that, then build. Say in the Task comment which of the two it was. Expected results come from the Spec 
              rule
              cited, never from running the code.
            - **Every Invariant the Task owns gets an oracle or case.** `conformance/not-yet.ts` lists the Invariants 
              each
              Task owns. The requirement lines are the ones with design content; every other owned Invariant still needs
              its oracle or case and a wrong runtime, written from its Spec text and its Spec test (T-numbered).
            - **Slice template** (T18, T19, T20; @epic.V3 instructions): the suite half (Spec change, model section, 
              parts,
              model mutation, audit) is green on the Spec model and reviewed before the production half starts; then
              production test-first; then the benchmark replay and the gates. "Declared, Enabled, Covered, Guards" in 
              each
              record's acceptance are that template's items and are kept verbatim.
            - **All fixtures are central** (the user, 2026-11-12). No check is "one app passes". Each requirement runs 
              over
              the generated parts available at its slice, under their usual and stress profiles, both world seeds:
              - "the base parts": the pilots S16 transfers, S21 sign-in and S10 sync 
                (`packages/flow-state-v3/fixtures/`),
                their app mixes (`MIX-*`), and the generated parts T52 makes of the hand fixtures (including Pay and
                checkout) and Cells;
              - built by T18, from
                code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/fixture-catalogue-2026-11-11.md: S01, S24 (composite A
                Editor); S08, S03, S22 flat (B Outbox); S12, S23 (C Control); S09 flat (G Optimistic);
              - built by T19: S13 (E Chat). S25 is @task.V3.T56's.

              A requirement line names the parts that reach it most directly; it still runs over all parts available. 
              The
              fixtures (including Pay and checkout) keep one more role: the type-error count against `src/index.ts`, 
              which
              checks the API, not behaviour.
            - **Store contents before the disk reader exists.** Until T19 builds the Port's disk reader, a check that 
              reads
              "store contents" reads the fake world's store from the harness side (`conformance/world/storage.ts`), 
              never
              through the runtime.
            - **Gates.** `nub run conformance:gates` runs them all and writes `conformance/gates.json`. Singly:
              `bun packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/runner/main.ts <mode>` with modes `suite`, `falsify`, `liar`, 
              `crash`
              (each `--runtime production`), `parts`, `pairs`, `matrix-gate`, `model-mutation`, `mutate` (core mutants),
              `coverage`, `regressions`. Then `nub run check:fix` and `nub run check` at zero errors and zero warnings.
              "No worse" means every gate's count at the Task's start, recorded in the Task's first comment.
            - **Benchmarks.** `packages/flow-state-v3/bench/bench.ts` replays a part and returns its work fingerprint 
              and
              turn costs; no runner mode calls it yet. A Task that needs a cost number adds the scenario there and runs 
              it
              alone (no other process), recording the command in its comment.
            - **Type tests** are `*.type-test.ts` files beside the code, using `@ts-expect-error`, checked by
              `nub run check:effect`; each is planted wrong once to see it fail for the expected reason.
            - **Shell.** Every command is bounded: `perl -e 'alarm N; exec @ARGV' -- <cmd> </dev/null`, under 3 
              minutes, no
              stdin reads; files are written with Write and Edit; kill your own stale shells. Suite slices and mutants 
              may
              run up to 8 in parallel; browsers and benchmarks run alone.
            - **Code style.** Read `tools/lint-bomb/STYLE.md`, `tools/lint-bomb/examples/style.ts` and
              `tools/lint-bomb/FLOW-STATE.md` before writing. The core (outside `src/host/`) is plain, pure, synchronous
              TypeScript returning `{ state, effects }`; the host uses Effect fully. No implicit state machine: a set of
              flags that together mean a state becomes one tagged value and one total table.
            - **Sidecars.** Haiku only, effort high, read-only unless given one disjoint file with an acceptance check; 
              the
              lead reads every line and re-runs every check. A reviewer sidecar checks each oracle, harness or runtime
              change.
            - **Traps that apply to every Task:**
              1. **A suite that cannot load once counted as killing mutants.** Fixed in `974ad3a1`: a kill counts only 
                 when
                 a failure names an oracle or a cited Invariant. Keep it guarded: for each new wrong runtime, read the
                 failure and confirm it names the oracle or Invariant its requirement lists. A load failure, defect,
                 timeout or missing verdict is a broken mutant, not a kill.
              2. **Which side is wrong.** A red on production may be the model's or the oracle's fault (T47 found four 
                 such:
                 GEN-pay-search-20, 21, 55 and seeded-173). Before changing production to turn a check green, derive the
                 expected result from the Spec rule by hand. If the model is wrong, fix the model and say so in the 
                 comment.
              3. **A lenient oracle passes everything.** Each oracle change needs its wrong runtime red before and 
                 after.
              4. **A differential baseline that silently re-records.** A baseline is re-recorded only with a named entry
                 that cites the pending case it flips.
            - **Never decide alone, in any Task:** a guarantee change (any Spec `must` or Invariant), a public name or 
              shape
              in `src/index.ts`, a new report kind, a new record kind or field, dropping or weakening a check, taking a
              documented fallback. Ask the orchestrator. Small names inside the core are the builder's.
            - **Close-out.** Each Task ends with a comment listing: gates at start and end, each allowed baseline 
              difference
              with its pending case, each Spec line changed (also in `workflow/specs/spec-log.md`), and the handoff 
              items
              written as comments on the receiving Task.
            - **Names.** A name after "Wrong:" is the name to give a new wrong runtime or mutant; none exists yet. An 
              oracle
              or case marked (new) is written by the Task; one marked (exists) is in 
              `packages/flow-state-v3/conformance/`
              today. File names for new code inside `src/core/` and `src/host/` are the builder's unless given. Bare 
              rule
              ids carry their Spec's name on every line (WriteLanes I31, Actors T21); never drop it. If two lines 
              disagree,
              or a fact is missing, stop and ask the orchestrator; do not choose.
        comment     2026-11-12T10:35:03Z
            Requirements (from core-task-briefs-2026-11-12.md), each red first

            - **R-1.** Restore reads bytes, never memory (Restore R1). **Check:** oracle `restore` (new): boot a fresh
              Runtime that shares only the disk reader's bytes with the writer, and compare what it hears with the 
              model.
              **Wrong:** `restore.reads-memory`.
            - **R-2.** After ready, the live incarnations restored from records are exactly the Owing identities whose
              actor record restores at that boot; every other record is still only a record; a boot reports no delivery
              (Restore I1; Restore T1; Actors T21). **Check:** new case. **Wrong:** `boot.delivers`,
              `boot.wakes-plain-record`.
            - **R-3.** At Start: the identity's owed rows in owed order; then, lane by lane in record-key order, each 
              queued
              commit's NotSent { Restored }, then the Held head's unknown { Restored } once, unless a row for its 
              Attempt is
              owed; all before any external event (Restore R3, I5, I6, I7). **Check:** reload cases on S08, S03. 
              **Wrong:**
              `start.lanes-before-rows`, `start.restored-twice`, `start.event-before-restore`.
            - **R-4.** An Attempt acknowledged once restores Held and ends unknown { Restored } with the same id; one 
              never
              acknowledged restores as never sent; no restore re-runs a turn or re-sends (Restore R3, I2, I8; WriteAhead
              I17). **Check:** new cases cutting before and after the acknowledgement lands (the `crash` gate). 
              **Wrong:**
              `restore.unacked-as-held`, `restore.resends`.
            - **R-5.** unknown { Restored } is in no record (Restore R3). **Check:** the disk reader over every part.
              **Wrong:** `record.stores-restored`.
            - **R-6.** A disposed identity's rows and unbound lanes wait for an adopting ensure and are owed at that 
              Start
              (Actors R20; Restore R1). **Check:** the regression `GEN-pay-search-25` (exists) stays green, plus a new
              production case. **Wrong:** `restore.drops-unbound-held`.
            - **R-7.** One record failing to decode, or a key of no kind, is quarantined with its bytes unchanged, 
              reported
              RecordUndecodable (proposed name), and never stops another record restoring or ready succeeding (Restore 
              R5,
              I10, I11; Codec R5). **Check:** oracle `quarantine` (new). **Wrong:** `restore.coerces-bad-record`,
              `restore.one-bad-record-fails-ready`.
            - **R-8.** A lane whose key no longer decodes or whose operation is gone is quarantined: it keeps its hold, 
              a
              commit on its key declines NotSent { Rejected }, and a cancel clears it (Restore R8, I13, I15). 
              **Check:** new
              case. **Wrong:** `restore.quarantined-lane-admits`.
            - **R-9.** The codec: bounds, canonical bytes, strict decoding, failures (Codec I1 to I6; Codec T1 to T5).
              **Check:** vectors written from the Spec. **Wrong:** one wrong codec per test, named `codec.t1` to
              `codec.t5`.
            - **R-10.** Every record has `format`, and each kind round-trips its Schema (Codec I7; Codec T6). **Check:**
              over every part. **Wrong:** `record.no-format`.
            - **R-11.** An identity's forget removes its actor and owed records and closes its rows, and keeps a Held
              lane's record (Actors R21). **Check:** new case with the disk reader. **Wrong:** `forget.leaves-owed`,
              `forget.drops-held-lane`.
            - **R-12.** `dispose({ forget: true })` resolves success only after deletion is confirmed; a failed deletion
              reports StorageFailure (Restore R10, I18). **Check:** new case on a store that refuses the deletion.
              **Wrong:** `forget.claims-success`.
            - **R-13.** After a read failure no write to that scope happens in that Runtime, and a storage failure never
              changes committed live state (Restore R9, I16, I17). **Check:** new cases with a storage spy. **Wrong:**
              `restore.writes-after-read-failure`.
            - **R-14.** Reload equivalence: at every crash point, the reports and snapshots after the boot equal the
              uncrashed run's (WriteAhead I21). **Check:** the `crash` gate over every part. **Wrong:**
              `restore.skips-owed-record`.

    T56
        title       Durability: release versions, key fingerprints and record migration (slice 2b)
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@task.V3.T21]
        satisfies   [@plan.Persistence.O5, @plan.Runtime.O3]
        description
            Build release versions on production: key fingerprints, identity versions, saved-shape resets with record 
            migration, and `previous` holds checked against Restore R6 (slice 2b).

            **Why now:** an upgraded release must reset or keep each saved record correctly. Split from @task.V3.T20 
            (orchestrator, 2026-11-12, wide consult W3) because a saved shape is defined only once @task.V3.T17 and 
            @task.V3.T57 fix private and computed fields and fragments, and identity versions need @task.V3.T9's 
            value-named actors; built before them it would be built on Pay-shaped machines and rebuilt.

            **Read first:** @spec.Restore, @spec.Codec, @task.V3.T20 (the restore it extends), 
            code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/fable-wide-consult-2026-11-12.md (W3, item 2).

            Absorbs @task.V3.T26 (record migration, @plan.Persistence.O5; real storage shapes are IndexedDB structured 
            clone from @task.V3.T58) and @task.V3.T11's migration wrong runtimes. Codes: up pg. Part built: S25 [A]. 
            Bench: the S25 upgrade boot. Blocked by @task.V3.T21 for the slice order (W3, item 4).

            **Phase-out, step 5 (the user accepted 2026-11-11):** the Stamp twin (release 1 and 2 keys; `up`) is 
            written in `fixtures/`; run the hand cases on it on production; write production mutants per oracle the 
            slice judges, mapped by Invariant from the round-14 mutants; delete `stamp.ts` once the derived reference 
            equals it. Read code:workflow/plans/flow/references/proposals/conformance-phase-out-2026-11-11.md.

            - **May change:** `src/`, the conformance fixtures, model and generator.
            - **Evidence:** this Task's comments.

            False pass: a reset judged from the runtime's report, not the bytes → caught by the restore oracle and the 
            Stamp twin's round-14 verdicts.
            Gates: the suite; falsify; the matrix and combination gates; `nub run check`.
            Stops: a guarantee change.
        acceptance
            The Restore area passes on production in full, with every Restore Invariant's oracle or case killing its 
            mutants.
            - Record migration passes cases on production: a changed saved shape resets its definition's records with 
              the writes in flight kept (from @task.V3.T26, @plan.Persistence.O5).
            - Key fingerprints, identity versions and `previous: [{ key }]` holds pass cases on production (Restore R6).
            - The slice's generated runs pass on production, and its benchmark replay matches the work fingerprint.
            - Declared: up pg are in the production and Spec-model capability lists, and the feature-matrix gate shows 
              none of them as owed.
            - Enabled: S25 [A] and the parts built earlier declare those codes among their needs, the generator draws 
              their operations, and the app mix runs them.
            - Covered: the combination gate counts the pairs among implemented codes, fails an unexercised interacting 
              pair with no dated waiver, and passes before this Task closes.
            - Guards: production's first-contact reds are recorded and classified, held-out seeds pass, and a sample of 
              the model's expectations is read by a different model family (Fable).
            - Phase-out step 5: the Stamp twin exists in `fixtures/` and `stamp.ts` is deleted; the same verdict per 
              case on round-14 and production.
            - Phase-out checks (the four validations): no lost catch in the catch matrix; the same coverage and 
              feature-matrix rows; the derived reference equal to the hand rows wherever both exist; Compile static 
              cases green on each fixture's `Compiled`.
